# HydraFlow Environment Variables
# `make setup` copies this file to .env when none exists (cp .env.sample .env).
#
# The QUICK START block below is everything most installs need. Everything
# under "Advanced" is optional tuning. Pipeline stages use one combo var each:
#   tool  ∈ {claude, codex, gemini, pi}
#   model ∈ tool-specific model slug
# Legacy per-knob vars (labels, tiered-policy, *_TOOL/*_MODEL) live in code
# defaults now — edit src/config.py if you need to change them.

# ============================================================================
# QUICK START — the only values most installs need
# ============================================================================

# 1) REQUIRED — the GitHub repo the factory works on. Without it HydraFlow
#    boots looking healthy and does NOTHING: the triage/plan/implement/review
#    loops idle forever. This is the one value you must set.
# HYDRAFLOW_GITHUB_REPO=owner/repo

# 2) GitHub auth — usually nothing to set here. `make setup` runs
#    `gh auth login`, and HydraFlow uses the gh CLI's stored auth by default.
#    Set a token only to OVERRIDE that (resolution order:
#    HYDRAFLOW_GH_TOKEN → GH_TOKEN → GITHUB_TOKEN; process env first, then
#    this file):
# HYDRAFLOW_GH_TOKEN=ghp_...

# 3) Claude auth
#    Host mode (default): nothing to set — your logged-in Claude CLI is used.
#    Docker mode (HYDRAFLOW_EXECUTION_MODE=docker): containers cannot reach
#    the host keychain. Generate a headless token and paste it here:
#        claude setup-token
# CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN=sk-ant-oat01-...
#    Or use an API key instead of a subscription token:
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=sk-ant-...

# 4) Other providers — needed only if a stage below names that tool:
#      gemini → GEMINI_API_KEY (https://aistudio.google.com/apikey)
#      codex  → nothing here; ~/.codex/ is mounted from the host
#      z.ai   → ZAI_API_KEY or ZAI_CODING_PLAN_KEY
#    Also honored when present: OPENAI_API_KEY, OPENROUTER_API_KEY,
#    XAI_API_KEY, DEEPSEEK_API_KEY, MISTRAL_API_KEY, TOGETHER_API_KEY,
#    GROQ_API_KEY, MOONSHOT_API_KEY, KIMI_API_KEY, PERPLEXITY_API_KEY,
#    AZURE_OPENAI_API_KEY + AZURE_OPENAI_ENDPOINT, GOOGLE_API_KEY.
# GEMINI_API_KEY=...

# ============================================================================
# Advanced — everything below is optional
# ============================================================================

# --- GitHub identity ---
# HYDRAFLOW_GITHUB_ASSIGNEE=username      # read by the hf.* slash commands, not the runtime
# HYDRAFLOW_GIT_USER_NAME=T-rav-HydraFlow-Ops
# HYDRAFLOW_GIT_USER_EMAIL=t@koderex.dev

# --- Pipeline stages (tool:model per stage) ---
# Defaults shown — uncomment to override.
# HYDRAFLOW_IMPLEMENT=claude:opus
# HYDRAFLOW_PLANNER=claude:opus
# HYDRAFLOW_REVIEW=claude:sonnet
# HYDRAFLOW_TRIAGE=gemini:gemini-3.1-pro-preview
# HYDRAFLOW_AC=claude:sonnet
# verification_judge has no env var — it auto-syncs to review_tool
# (they share review_model).

# --- Background workers ---
# HYDRAFLOW_TRANSCRIPT_SUMMARY=claude:haiku
# HYDRAFLOW_WIKI_COMPILATION=claude:haiku
# HYDRAFLOW_REPORT_ISSUE=claude:opus
# HYDRAFLOW_ADR_REVIEW=claude:sonnet      # ADR review panel orchestrator
#
# NOTE: the sentry-triage and code-grooming worker models are config-file /
# settings-UI fields, not env vars — no override table reads HYDRAFLOW_SENTRY
# or HYDRAFLOW_CODE_GROOMING. (An earlier revision of this file documented
# both, plus a migration note; setting them did nothing.)

# --- Meta (inherit cascades to every per-stage default) ---
# HYDRAFLOW_SYSTEM=inherit
# HYDRAFLOW_BACKGROUND=inherit            # cascades to background-worker defaults

# --- Alternative profile: codex-heavy (uncomment block to activate) ---
# HYDRAFLOW_IMPLEMENT=codex:gpt-5-codex
# HYDRAFLOW_PLANNER=claude:opus
# HYDRAFLOW_REVIEW=claude:sonnet
# HYDRAFLOW_TRIAGE=gemini:gemini-3.1-pro-preview

# --- Terminal gateway fleet profile (enable only after gateway deploy) ---
# With the ratchet enabled, every untouched gateway-capable provider dial is
# promoted to `gateway`; any explicitly configured direct Claude/z.ai harness
# value fails config loading. One-shot HTTP providers remain an intentional
# exception. Workers receive per-spawn virtual keys, never provider/admin keys.
# HYDRAFLOW_GATEWAY_BASE_URL=http://gateway:8080
# Mint the control token in the canonical `hfgwctl_` grammar so `secret_scrub`
# can redact it from the audit stream even when it appears without its name
# (gateway/README.md; ADR-0085, issue #11635):
#   python -c "import secrets; print('hfgwctl_' + secrets.token_urlsafe(32))"
# HYDRAFLOW_GATEWAY_CONTROL_TOKEN=hfgwctl_replace-with-a-freshly-minted-secret0
# HYDRAFLOW_GATEWAY_FLEET_RATCHET_ENABLED=true
# HYDRAFLOW_EXECUTION_MODE=docker            # required: isolates host OAuth/keychains
# A staged canary can instead set one role while leaving the ratchet disabled:
# HYDRAFLOW_ADR_REVIEW_PROVIDER=gateway

# --- Routing enforcement canary (#11539, ADR-0141) ---
# Names the ONE canonical owner/repo whose gateway-routed spawns are bound by
# the routing policy resolver instead of by the legacy dials. Empty (the
# default) enforces nothing anywhere, and clearing it is the one-action
# rollback: it takes effect on the next spawn, with no restart and without
# touching the policy snapshot. It never promotes a spawn onto the gateway —
# only traffic already transiting the tap can be bound, so a repository with
# neither the ratchet nor a gateway dial has nothing to enforce.
# HYDRAFLOW_GATEWAY_ENFORCEMENT_CANARY_REPO=acme/project-x
#
# This dial is deliberately NOT read from the environment — it is a config-file
# and settings-UI field only. HydraFlow's env overrides apply whenever a field is
# at its default, and the disarmed value IS the default, so an env var here would
# mean clearing the dial did not disarm anything. The line above shows the value,
# not a supported override.
#
# The gateway deployable owns the other half of the boundary, and it IS an env
# var — on the gateway process, not this one (gateway/README.md):
#   GATEWAY_GOVERNED_REPOS=acme/project-x
# While it names a repository, that repository's v1 mints are refused and its
# unbound keys are turned away, so a caller cannot declare itself ungoverned.
# Empty by default. Arm the spawn side first and the gateway second; disarm the
# gateway last — clearing only the HydraFlow dial while the gateway still governs
# the repository leaves its spawns failing closed at the mint.

# --- Gateway multi-account pool (#11540, ADR-0142) ---
# Three more env vars on the GATEWAY process, not this one (gateway/README.md).
# All are optional: unset, the deployment behaves exactly as it does today.
#   GATEWAY_ACCOUNTS_FILE=/etc/hydraflow-gateway/accounts.yaml
# The server-owned document declaring accounts beyond ADR-0138's two compiled
# legacy ones. Unset by default, and that default IS the pre-pool deployment:
# exactly those two accounts, one candidate per model, and nowhere for a
# fallback hop to go. Restart-required on purpose — an account's credential and
# origin are deployment facts, and reloading them under live leases would let a
# key minted against one origin be served by another.
# The file never carries a credential: an account names the VARIABLE that
# configures it (`credential_env`), and that name must live in the `GATEWAY_`
# namespace, because the `GATEWAY_` prefix is exactly what HydraFlow's worker
# environment scrub (`subprocess_util.scrub_gateway_spawn_env`) strips. A name
# outside that namespace would be inherited by every routed worker, so it is
# refused at load rather than quietly accepted.
#   GATEWAY_ACCOUNT_STATE_DIR=/var/lib/hydraflow-gateway/accounts
# Where the audited administrative overlay and its hash chain are written.
# Defaults to `.hydraflow/gateway/accounts`; give it the same durable,
# non-root-writable mount discipline as GATEWAY_LEDGER_PATH.
#   GATEWAY_MAX_FALLBACK_HOPS=1
# The hard ceiling on bounded fallback — a ceiling, not a target: the effective
# bound is always the smaller of this and the candidate list, so a pool can
# never be walked in a loop. Default 1, the shipped one-primary/one-backup
# story. `0` refuses every fallback HOP: a request is served by its first
# eligible candidate or held there. Lost-response recovery is unaffected — a
# `supersedes` citation re-mints at the same position and spends no budget.

# --- Scheduling model (#11535, ADR-0137) ---
# Two orthogonal dials. Default is Classic: today's behaviour, unchanged.
# 'issue_controller' runs one fenced IssueDriver per issue over the same stage
# workers; 'fable_director' additionally attaches a SHADOW Fable director that
# records what it would dispatch while the driver still executes everything.
# Both are restart-required — the orchestrator picks its pipeline loops at boot.
# HYDRAFLOW_SCHEDULING_MODEL=phase_requeue   # phase_requeue | issue_controller
# HYDRAFLOW_EXECUTION_RUNTIME=stage_subprocess  # stage_subprocess | fable_director

# --- Fable director, shadow mode (#11537, ADR-0137 S1-S6) ---
# Read only under HYDRAFLOW_EXECUTION_RUNTIME=fable_director. No production
# worker is dispatched by the director in this mode; it observes and records.
# A turn over its budget is killed with its whole process group and recorded
# as failed. At budget 0.0 every requested dispatch is refused as
# BUDGET_EXHAUSTED, which observes intent while admitting nothing.
# HYDRAFLOW_DIRECTOR_TURN_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=180
# HYDRAFLOW_DIRECTOR_SHADOW_USD_BUDGET=1.0
# Kill switch (live) and the AGGREGATE spend bound for the whole run. The
# budget above is per-boundary and bounds what a director may REQUEST; the
# ceiling below bounds what the director itself may SPEND.
# HYDRAFLOW_DIRECTOR_SHADOW_ENABLED=true
# HYDRAFLOW_DIRECTOR_SHADOW_USD_CEILING=25.0
#
# NOTE (#11541): the four HYDRAFLOW_DIRECTOR_* names above are documented here
# but are not read from the environment — no override table carries them, so
# uncommenting them does nothing. They are config-file and settings-UI fields.
# Flagged rather than silently left; wiring them is a separate change.

# --- Fable Plan canary (#11541, ADR-0137 P3) ---
# Names the ONE canonical owner/repo whose PLAN boundaries may dispatch real
# brokered Sonnet/Opus workers. Empty (the default) dispatches nothing anywhere;
# clearing it is the one-action rollback and takes effect on the next boundary.
# Requires HYDRAFLOW_EXECUTION_RUNTIME=fable_director as well — selecting the
# observer and trusting the actuator are two separate operator decisions.
#
# This dial is deliberately NOT read from the environment, for the same reason
# HYDRAFLOW_GATEWAY_ENFORCEMENT_CANARY_REPO is not (ADR-0141 D5): an env
# override applies whenever a field is at its default, and the disarmed value
# IS the default — so an env var would mean clearing the dial in the config file
# or the settings UI disarmed nothing. It is a config-file and settings-UI field
# only. The line below shows the value, not a supported override.
#   fable_plan_canary_repo=acme/project-x
# fable_plan_worker_timeout_seconds is likewise config-file/settings-UI only.

# --- Fable Implement canary (#11542, ADR-0137 P4) ---
# The SECOND dial, and deliberately not a widening of the first: naming a
# repository here lets its IMPLEMENT boundaries dispatch brokered Sonnet
# implementer and Opus/Sonnet debugger workers. A write-capable worker holds
# the issue worktree's single writer lease, and its result is admitted only
# while the branch, base SHA, HEAD and diff digest it was briefed against are
# still the live ones — a superseded worker gets a receipt, not a merge.
# Arming this arms nothing about the Plan canary, and vice versa.
#
# Not read from the environment, for the same reason the block above is not.
#   fable_implement_canary_repo=acme/project-x
# fable_implement_worker_timeout_seconds is likewise config-file/settings-UI only.

# --- Runtime / host ---
# HYDRAFLOW_EXECUTION_MODE=host              # host | docker
# HYDRAFLOW_DASHBOARD_HOST=127.0.0.1
# HYDRAFLOW_DASHBOARD_PORT=5555              # backend; the dev UI proxies it on :5556
# (The dashboard itself is ON by default — `dashboard_enabled` is a
#  config-file/settings-UI field, not an env var.)

# --- Operator auth ---
# Gates operator-authenticated dashboard routes; also required by the Bugsink
# intake proxy (docker-compose.intake-proxy.yml).
# HYDRAFLOW_OPERATOR_TOKEN=...
# Overrides a configured SENTRY_DSN, always toward OFF.
# HYDRAFLOW_SENTRY_DISABLED=1
# Where the intake fetches a group's stack trace from (pairs with
# BUGSINK_API_TOKEN). Without it triage classifies on one line of message, and
# a sensor issue that FAILS triage is auto-closed — thin evidence discards bugs.
# HYDRAFLOW_BUGSINK_BASE_URL=http://localhost:8000
#
# Intake proxy, remote deploys only (make bugsink-up-exposed). Keep
# HYDRAFLOW_DASHBOARD_HOST on loopback: the dashboard has no in-process auth on
# most routes (ADR-0138 §D5), so publishing it exposes the control plane, not
# the UI. Only the two lanes below are ever reachable.
# HF_EXCEPTION_PATH_TOKEN=        # openssl rand -hex 32
# HF_REPORT_PATH_TOKEN=           # openssl rand -hex 32
# HF_TLS_CERT_DIR=/etc/letsencrypt/live/<host>   # holds fullchain.pem + privkey.pem

# --- Sentry error tracking ---
# SENTRY_DSN=https://<key>@o<org>.ingest.us.sentry.io/<project>
# (SENTRY_AUTH_TOKEN / SENTRY_ORG were removed — nothing reads them.)

# --- Bugsink exception sensor (ADR-0146; `make bugsink-up`) ---
# The compose files refuse to start without the first three.
# BUGSINK_SECRET_KEY=                        # openssl rand -base64 50
# BUGSINK_SUPERUSER=email:password
# BUGSINK_DB_PASSWORD=...
# BUGSINK_API_TOKEN=...                      # read by the runtime's exception sensor
# BUGSINK_PORT=8000
# BUGSINK_BASE_URL=http://localhost:8000

# (The OTel/Honeycomb block that used to live here is gone: ADR-0055 is
#  Superseded by ADR-0118 — OTel was removed from the codebase entirely, and
#  none of those vars were read.)

# --- Two-tier branch model (ADR-0042) ---
# When true, agent PRs target the staging branch; StagingPromotionLoop cuts
# rc/YYYY-MM-DD-HHMM PRs into main on rc_cadence_hours cadence (default 4h).
# When false, every PR targets main directly (pre-feature behaviour).
# HYDRAFLOW_STAGING_ENABLED=true
# Branch names — defaults match GitHub conventions; change only if you've
# renamed the branches in your fork.
# HYDRAFLOW_MAIN_BRANCH=main
# HYDRAFLOW_STAGING_BRANCH=staging
# RC promotion cadence — every N hours, the loop tries to cut a fresh rc/* PR
# (only if there are commits on staging that aren't already in an open RC).
# HYDRAFLOW_RC_CADENCE_HOURS=4
# Inner loop tick interval (seconds). Lower = faster reaction to PR-CI events.
# HYDRAFLOW_STAGING_PROMOTION_INTERVAL=300
# Stale rc/* branch sweep retention. Closed/merged RC branches older than this
# get pruned by the loop's daily sweep.
# HYDRAFLOW_STAGING_RC_RETENTION_DAYS=7

